WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.980
In assessing past school reforms, it's
all too easy to find glossy PR or ad

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.870
hominem critiques. Much harder to find
are analyses that tease out practical

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:19.820
lessons. In our new book, we identify
two. One: It's easy to forget that

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.260
political timelines are different than
educational ones. Elections breed a sense

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:29.180
of urgency among policymakers, and a
desire for rapid visible change, as with

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.920
President Obama's Race to the Top. Yet,
rebooting education policies, revamping

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.050
instructional materials, and changing
school cultures is a slower process. Two:

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:43.220
It is tough to course-correct
once high profile reforms pick up

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.030
momentum. By the time that ambitious
efforts like President Bush's No Child

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.409
Left Behind had disappointed and started
to fuel backlash, their champions were

00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:56.119
too emotionally and politically invested
to readily alter course. There are

00:00:56.119 --> 00:01:00.019
real constraints on how effective policy
will be when it comes to driving school

00:01:00.019 --> 00:01:04.640
improvement. That suggests skepticism may
be warranted when eyeing the grand

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.560
educational promises of those vying for
office.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.380
What do you think about the

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.500
school reforms of Presidents Bush and
Obama? Let us know in our poll. Also, let

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:18.950
us know what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:25.479
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

